{"name":"Laughing Orange","hex":"#f49807","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"laughing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["verbal noun of laugh: the action of the verb to laugh."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of laugh"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/laughing"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Laughter","blocks":[{"text":"Laughter is a typically pleasant physical reaction and emotion consisting usually of rhythmical, usually audible contractions of the diaphragm and other parts of the respiratory system. It is a response to certain external or internal stimuli. Laughter can rise from such stimuli as being tickled, or from humorous stories, imagery, videos or thoughts. Most commonly, it is considered an auditory expression of a number of positive emotional states, such as joy, mirth, happiness or relief. On some occasions, however, it may be caused by contrary emotional states such as embarrassment, surprise, or confusion such as nervous laughter or courtesy laugh. Age, gender, education, language and culture are all indicators as to whether a person will experience laughter in a given situation. Other than humans, some other species of primate express laughter-like vocalizations in response to physical contact such as wrestling, play chasing or tickling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Laughing_boy_at_golden_hour.jpg/330px-Laughing_boy_at_golden_hour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laughter"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg/500px-Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg/1280px-Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg/500px-Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A young girl from Laos laughing while hugging her dog","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuman%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipe